<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Balkans and Hungary</p><h1 class="st-title">Principality of Albania (medieval)</h1><p class="st-meta">1358 CE – 1392 CE (approx.) · capital Durrës</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Principality of the Thopia family centred on Durrës, a leading Albanian realm of the late 14th century that ended when Durrës passed to Venice.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-thopia">Karl Thopia</a>, an Albanian noble claiming Angevin descent through his mother, built up lands between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mat-river">Mat</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shkumbin">Shkumbin</a> rivers. In 1368 he took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durres">Durrës</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capetian-house-of-anjou">Angevins</a>, ending the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-albania-medieval">Kingdom of Albania (medieval)</a>, and styled himself Prince of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/albania">Albania</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Karl ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durres">Durrës</a> and the inland town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbasan">Elbasan</a>, rivalling <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/zeta-under-the-balsici">Zeta under the Balšići</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muzaka-family">Muzaka of Berat</a>. He founded the monastery of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monastery-of-saint-john-vladimir">Shën Gjon Vladimir</a> near Elbasan. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balsa-ii">Balša II</a> seized Durrës in 1385, and Karl called in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>, who killed Balša at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-savra">Savra</a>; Karl recovered the city soon after.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Karl died in 1388. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gjergj-thopia">Gjergj Thopia</a>, weakened by Ottoman pressure, handed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durres">Durrës</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> in 1392, and he died soon after. The remaining <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thopia-family">Thopia</a> lands later fell under Ottoman control.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/principality-of-albania-medieval.jpg" alt="Principality of Albania (medieval)" width="320" height="401"><figcaption>Image: Killerdani12 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:All_lands_held_by_the_Principality_of_Albania_(1359%E2%80%931415).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1358 CE – 1392 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Durrës</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Karl Thopia, Gjergj Thopia</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity; Orthodox Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Albanian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Albania</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Thopia principality, Principality of Albania (Thopia)</dd></dl></aside></div>
